Overview
Over five weeks I went from asking "what is a robot?" to building a simulated robotic arm that works in two modes. Each week added one piece: the brain, the code, the muscles, the senses, and wireless control. The capstone combined all of them.
Week 1: Foundations of Robotics and Arduino
• A robot senses, decides and acts. A robotic arm is a robot built to reach, grip and place things.
• A microcontroller is a whole computer on one chip, with a processor, memory and input/output pins. It is the brain of the robot.
• Arduino is an ecosystem: the board (such as the Uno), the chip on it (the ATmega328P), external hardware, and the free Arduino IDE.
• A digital pin is either HIGH (5V, on) or LOW (0V, off).
• I wrote my first program, Blink, and learned the workflow: simulate in Tinkercad first, then upload to hardware, then commit to GitHub.
Week 2: Programming for Robotics
• A sketch is an Arduino program. Every sketch has setup(), which runs once, and loop(), which runs forever.
• Variables and data types (int, long, float, bool, char) store values, and constants lock them in place.
• digitalWrite() drives outputs, and digitalRead() reads inputs.
• INPUT_PULLUP keeps a pin HIGH until a button pulls it LOW, so the logic is inverted: pressed reads LOW.
• The Serial Monitor (Serial.begin(9600) and Serial.println()) lets me watch what the robot is thinking, which is the best debugging tool.
• I built the Blink, Button-LED, and traffic light projects, using delay() to sequence the lights.
Week 3: Actuators and Servo Motion
• An actuator turns the controller's decisions into movement. Servos are closed-loop: a motor, gears, a potentiometer and a control board that keep checking the real angle against the target.
• myservo.write(angle) sends an angle from 0 to 180, and the Servo library turns it into the right pulse.
• A servo signal runs at 50 Hz (a pulse every 20 ms). The pulse width (about 1 ms to 2 ms) sets the angle. Frequency and pulse width are not the same thing.
• The read, map, write pattern lets a potentiometer control a servo: analogRead(), then map(), then write().
• Servos need their own power supply with a shared ground, and a PCA9685 driver helps scale up to many joints.
• A 6-DOF arm has six joints (base, shoulder, elbow, wrist pitch, wrist roll, gripper), with stronger servos on the joints that fight gravity.
Week 4: Sensors and Communication
• The HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor sends a 40 kHz sound pulse and times the echo.
• Distance (cm) = duration (microseconds) x 0.034 / 2. I divide by 2 because the sound travels to the object and back.
• The Trig pin gets a 10 microsecond HIGH pulse, and pulseIn(ECHO, HIGH) measures how long Echo stays HIGH.
• Sensors are not perfect. A reading of 0 means no echo, and anything beyond about 400 cm is unreliable, so the code should fail safely instead of trusting a bad number.
• The sensor and the Arduino must share a common ground.
Week 5: Meet the Robotic Arm
• Serial communication sends data one bit at a time. TX connects to RX, and both sides must use the same baud rate.
• The HC-05 Bluetooth module is a wireless serial bridge. To the Arduino it looks like a cable.
• A command protocol gives meaning to characters, so that '1' can mean "turn on" or 'S1:90' can mean "move joint 1 to 90 degrees".
• The command chain is phone, Bluetooth, HC-05, Arduino, then servo. It follows the same read, decide, act pattern as before.
• Practical lessons: unplug D0 and D1 when uploading, match baud rates, and protect the HC-05's 3.3V RXD pin.
Capstone: Autonomous and Manual Robotic Arm
The capstone used a microcontroller, an ultrasonic sensor, a buzzer, potentiometers and servo motors. I built it in Tinkercad. It has two modes, selected with a push button.
